{"thinking": "The scheduler log lists six fine-tune jobs for six different customers. All six start at day 22.0 and end at day 28.3, and together they cover all 1,100 scheduled GPUs on A and B. Each job sits just under 50,000 GPU-hours (26,425 or 30,200), but the total is about 166,000. The grid data matches this timing.

The identical start and end times and the even split are signs that one run may have been labelled as six jobs (H3). The honest explanation is six unrelated customers who happened to be scheduled at the same moment.

The interconnect tap on the A-B link is my own instrument, so the operator cannot fake it. If the jobs on A and the jobs on B are separate, almost no traffic should cross between clusters. If one coordinated run spans both clusters, I would expect heavy cross-cluster traffic: about 1 GB/s per GPU for ordinary training, or about 10 MB/s per GPU for training built to exchange less. I'll tap the A-B link on day 24, in the middle of the window.", "action": "interconnect_tap", "args": {"link": "A-B", "day": 24}}
